5. **Job titles.** Enter each role using LinkedIn's dropdown where possible so the titles are searchable. The current role should read 'Business Coach - Construction and Allied Trades' or 'The P.R.O.F.I.T. Coach' depending on which drives more relevant search traffic. Check that at least one role includes 'business coach' for discoverability.

> **Discreet version available:** This profile is written for public visibility, where Pam can be clearly identified as a business coach. If at any point a discreet version is needed - for example during a transition period or where a non-compete applies - a version positioning Pam as an operator and adviser, without the word 'coach', is available on request.

===== campaigns:messaging (71s, 10385 chars)
Every message in this campaign is built on one truth: Steve is putting decent turnover through and still staring at a bank account that does not reflect it. Pam's proof, her 43 years in business and 8 years in the trade, earns the right to say that out loud.

- **Core client:** MDs of groundworks, civils, drainage and allied trades businesses in Yorkshire, £1.5M to £8M turnover, 12 to 35 staff
- **Main desire:** Find out where the margin keeps going and stop sorting everything themselves
- **Proof used:** 43 years running businesses, 8 years working specifically with MDs in construction and allied trades, ranked world's number one business coach four consecutive years (2015 to 2018), John Davy testimonial (number still needed)
- **Proof type:** Career proof (primary). Client proof (supporting, number pending)

### Campaign A: Connector

**Connection request**

```text
Hi {first_name}, I see you run a Yorkshire groundworks or civils business. Are you looking to find out where the margin goes and actually keep more of what you earn? I ask because in 8 years working with MDs in the trade I've used a simple system to find the profit leak and fix it. Is this of interest?
```

_Send with the connection request. 272 characters. Under the 275-character limit._

**Message 1**

```text
Hi {first_name}, thanks for connecting.

In 8 years working specifically with MDs of groundworks, civils and allied trades businesses in Yorkshire, I have seen the same pattern over and over.

Decent turnover. Decent order book. But the margin that looked fine when the job was priced has gone somewhere between start and finish, and nobody can quite put their finger on where.

They are sorting plant breakdowns and chasing late payments from main contractors instead of pricing the next six months of work. Every decision still lands on their phone. The contracts manager is a good lad but the MD is still doing half his job because it is quicker.

Busy. Grafting. But not enough to show for it.

That is the problem the PROFIT System removes. I have spent 43 years in business, the last 8 working specifically in the trade, and I was ranked the world's number one business coach four years running, measured on client results, not on how many people signed up.

And I believe with the right system in place, your business can do the same.

Are you looking to find out where the margin is going and actually keep more of what you earn? Would you be interested in learning more?
```

_Send 30 minutes after connecting._

**Message 2**

```text
Hi {first_name}, just checking this landed with you. No pressure at all. If the timing is off, that is fine. Worth a quick reply either way so I know where you are.
```

_Send 1 day after Message 1._

**Message 3**

```text
Hi {first_name}, I know you are probably getting messages from coaches every other week. Most of them will not have spent 43 years running real businesses or 8 years working specifically with MDs in groundworks and civils.

I am not going to promise a transformation or send you a worksheet. I am going to look at your numbers and find out which bit of the business is capping the profit, then we sort that first.

It never hurts to have a conversation. Even if it tells us both the timing is wrong.
```

_Send 2 days after Message 2._

**Message 4**

```text
Hi {first_name}, would you be open to a 20-minute call? No agenda other than a straight look at where the profit is going in your business. If there is nothing in it for you, I will tell you that on the call. If there is, we can talk about what it looks like to fix it.
```

_Send 4 days after Message 3._

**Message 5**

```text
Hi {first_name}, last one from me. If finding out where the margin goes and getting some of your time back is on your list this year, I am happy to have a quick chat whenever it suits. If the timing is genuinely not right, no hard feelings. I work with a small number of MDs in the trade and I am selective about who I take on. If that changes, feel free to come back to me.
```

_Send 2 weeks after Message 4._

### Open InMail

**Open InMail**

```text
Hi {first_name},

I work specifically with MDs of groundworks, civils, drainage and allied trades businesses across Yorkshire. A few things I see coming up again and again with businesses at your level:

1️⃣ The margin looked fine when the job was priced. By the time it finished, there was next to nowt in it.
2️⃣ Turnover is solid but the bank account at month-end tells a different story.
3️⃣ The contracts manager is a good lad but the MD is still doing half the job.

I have spent 43 years running businesses myself and the last 8 years working specifically with MDs in the trade. In that time I have seen exactly where the profit leaks and how to fix it, whether that is the pricing, the team structure, the cash flow or all three.

I have combined that with a proven method to create The Construction PROFIT System, which works on the five levers that actually move profit in a trade business: the jobs you win, the margin you keep, the team that runs it, the cash that flows, and the time you get back.

The problem most MDs in construction face is this: the business is too dependent on them for every decision, the margin is leaking somewhere they cannot pin down, and they are too busy sorting today's problems to stop and fix tomorrow's structure.

Most reach a point where the turnover is there but the reward is not. That crossroads is usually what starts the conversation.

I was ranked the world's number one business coach four consecutive years, from 2015 to 2018, measured on client results. One client told me: 'Her fees are completely outstripped by the money that you will get back.'

Interested in a straight conversation about where the profit is going in your business?
```

_Use for first-degree connections who have not responded, or for targeted outreach to MDs in construction where a connection request is not the right first step._

### Campaign B: Conversation into the BOSS Scorecard

**After connecting**

```text
Hi {first_name}, great to connect. Quick question, are you focused on growing the business right now, or more on protecting what you have already built?
```

_Send within the hour of connecting. Keep it short. One question only. Wait for the reply before sending anything else._

**Insight**

```text
That makes sense. Most MDs I speak to in the trade are somewhere in between, pushing for more turnover while trying to hold the margin together at the same time.

Here is what I find: every business has a weakest link, one thing that is quietly capping the profit more than anything else. Could be the pricing, the team, the cash flow, the way work comes in. Most owners have a hunch about which one it is. Most of the time the hunch is wrong, or it is a symptom of something else sitting underneath it.

The businesses that move fastest are the ones that find the actual root cause first, rather than fixing the thing that is making the most noise.
```

_Send once they have replied. Match their tone. If they are brief, be brief._

**Scorecard offer**

```text
There is a three-minute scorecard I use with MDs in construction and allied trades. It is called the BOSS Scorecard and it gives you a score out of 100 across the five areas that drive profit in a trade business: leads, conversion, job value, repeat work and margin. It does not give you a generic report. It shows you which area is capping your profit right now, so you know which bit to fix first rather than guessing.

Most MDs find it useful even if they never speak to me. It takes three minutes and it is free.
```

_Send once the insight message has landed and the conversation is moving. Do not rush to this step._

**Leave the link**

```text
Here is the link when you are ready: {assessment_url}

No sign-up process, no sales call at the end unless you want one. Just a straight score on where your business is right now. If it throws up anything worth talking through, I am happy to have a look at it with you.
```

_Send as a standalone message. No additional pitch. Let the scorecard do the work._

### Check before launch

| Check | Pass |
| --- | --- |
| 1. Under 275 characters | Yes. Connection request is 272 characters. |
| 2. Opens with Hi {first_name}, | Yes. All messages open with Hi {first_name}. |
| 3. Specific callout: I see you run a UK construction business | Yes. 'I see you run a Yorkshire groundworks or civils business.' |
| 4. Hook as a question: Are you looking to... | Yes. 'Are you looking to find out where the margin goes and actually keep more of what you earn?' |
| 5. I ask because | Yes. 'I ask because in 8 years working with MDs in the trade I've used a simple system...' |
| 6. A simple system | Yes. 'I've used a simple system to find the profit leak and fix it.' |
| 7. Specific proof with numbers and a timeframe | Fix. Career proof is present (8 years, 43 years, four consecutive world number one rankings) but no client result with a specific number. John Davy testimonial is used in the InMail but lacks a profit figure. See warning below. |
| 8. The mechanism tied to the hook | Yes. Finding the profit leak is tied directly to finding where the margin goes. |
| 9. Owner's language used throughout | Yes. Margin, jobs, the trade, lads, contracts manager, main contractors, pricing, turnover, nowt, graft. |
| 10. Interest CTA: Is this of interest? | Yes. Connection request closes with 'Is this of interest?' |

> **Campaign cannot launch at full strength until this number exists:** The John Davy testimonial is the strongest proof asset in the record: 'Her fees are completely outstripped by the money that you will get back.' It is used in the InMail and Message 1. But it carries no specific number, no profit figure, no timeframe, and no company count. Check 7 above flags this as a fix. To make the campaign as strong as it can be, add at least one of the following before launch: the approximate profit identified across John's companies (a range is fine, e.g. 'six figures across three businesses'), the timeframe of the coaching relationship, or the number of companies involved. Ask John directly or check session notes. Once that number exists, update Message 1 and the InMail to lead with it. Career proof alone is enough to run a test sequence, but client proof with a number will outperform it.

===== conversations:followup (70s, 9646 chars)
Most replies from Steve will not be a yes or a no. They will be a question, a deflection, or a one-liner. Each one is an opening.

### When they reply

**They say: Yes, interested**

```text
Good to hear, {first_name}. Quick question before I say anything else: is the main thing on your mind the margin disappearing between pricing and finishing, the team still depending on you for everything, or something else? I want to make sure I'm pointing you at the right thing first.
```

_Do not pitch. Ask one question. Find out where the pain sits before you move to next steps._

**They say: What is it exactly?**

```text
Plain and simple: I look at your numbers and find out which part of the business is capping the profit. Then we sort that bit first. No generic programme, no folder full of worksheets. We work on your jobs, your margin, your team. Most MDs I work with find the first few weeks free up hours rather than adding to the load. Are you happy for me to send you a three-minute scorecard that shows you where the leak is before we speak?
```

_Point to the BOSS Scorecard. Let it do the qualifying work._

**They say: How much is it?**

```text
Fair question, {first_name}. MDs I work with invest anywhere from under a thousand pounds a month up to fifty thousand pounds a year depending on what we are working on together. Most start at the lower end while we find the quick wins, and it builds from there. What most of them tell me is that the first leaks we find more than cover the cost. Worth a twenty-minute call to see if there is even anything in it for you before we talk numbers properly. I am free Tuesday or Thursday this week if either works.
```

_Use the anchor script. Name two days. Do not send a price list._

**They say: Not right now**

```text
No problem at all, {first_name}. Timing matters. Can I ask what right now looks like for you? If it is genuinely just a busy patch, I am happy to check back in. If there is something specific that would need to shift first, it helps me know whether it is worth keeping in touch.
```

_One question. Do not push. This reply opens the nurture sequence._

**They say: We are doing fine**

```text
Good to hear. Most MDs I speak to who are doing fine still find one or two leaks when they look properly. Sometimes it is margin, sometimes it is cash flow, sometimes it is just time. There is a three-minute scorecard that gives you a score out of 100 across the five areas that drive profit in a trade business. Worth doing just to confirm where you are. No strings. Want me to send the link?
```

_Do not argue with fine. Offer the scorecard as a check, not a challenge._

**They say: Who are you?**

```text
I am Pam Woodford. I started my first business at 17, spent 28 years building and running businesses myself, and for the last 8 years I have worked specifically with MDs of groundworks, civils and allied trades businesses in Yorkshire. I was ranked the world's number one business coach four years running, from 2015 to 2018, measured on client results. One of my clients put it better than I would: 'Her fees are completely outstripped by the money that you will get back.' I am based in Hull. I have no patience for theory that does not show up in the bank account. Does that help?
```

_Keep it tight. Career proof first, client proof second. Let them ask the next question._

**They say: [neutral one-liner, e.g. OK or Thanks]**

```text
Appreciate the reply, {first_name}. Can I ask one thing? Is finding out where the margin is going something that is on your radar this year, or is it further down the list right now?
```

_Neutral is not a no. One question reopens the conversation without pressure._

**They say: Send me some info**

```text
Will do. Rather than send you a brochure that tells you nothing useful, let me send you something that actually does a job. It is a three-minute scorecard called the BOSS Scorecard. It gives you a score out of 100 across the five areas that drive profit in a trade business and shows you which one is capping you right now. That will tell us both more than any PDF I could send. Here is the link: {assessment_url}. If anything comes up that is worth a look, I am happy to go through it with you on a quick call.
```

_Do not send a brochure. Send the scorecard. Follow up in two days if no response._

### Avoid

- Pitching the programme before you know what their problem is.
- Sending a wall of text when they gave you four words.
- Treating a neutral reply as a no and going quiet.
- Asking two questions in one message.
- Sending a price list when they ask about cost.
- Talking about the system before you have talked about them.
- Following up the same day twice.
- Making them feel stupid for not knowing which bit to fix.
- Using coaching language: journey, empower, unlock, transform.
- Overselling on a warm reply. Curiosity closes more than enthusiasm does.

### VIP nurture: top 100

> **Who goes on the top 100 list:** MDs of groundworks, civils, drainage or allied trades businesses in Yorkshire with a visible turnover of £1.5M or above who have connected, replied to a message, or engaged with a post in the last 90 days. Warm beats cold. Someone who said 'not right now' is closer to a yes than someone who has never heard of you. Send a personalised message every two weeks. Each one gives away something useful. Never sell on these messages.

**VIP nurture 1: Profit Maximiser**

```text
{personalisation}

I put this table together for MDs in the trade because it shows something most accountants never spell out clearly.

At a 10% net margin, if you cut your running costs by 11%, you double your profit. Not grow it. Double it.

The Profit Maximiser shows you exactly how that works across different margin levels, so you can see what a small reduction in the right costs actually does to the bottom line.

1️⃣ Takes ten minutes to fill in with your own numbers.
2️⃣ Shows you which costs are worth attacking first at your margin level.
3️⃣ Gives you a clear target before you pick up the phone to a supplier.

Here is the link: [link]

If it throws up a number worth talking through, I am happy to take a look with you. No agenda.

Pam
```

_Send as the first VIP nurture message. Follow up three days later with the five-step expense review: Does this cost help me get or keep customers? If not, it is a candidate for the list._

**VIP nurture 2: Cashflow Forecaster**

```text
{personalisation}

There is a saying I use with MDs in the trade: turnover is vanity, profit is sanity, cash is king.

Most groundworks businesses I work with have decent turnover on paper. The problem is the gap between finishing a job and getting paid for it. Two main contractors paying 60 days late in the same month can tip a £3M business into a genuinely uncomfortable position.

The Cashflow Forecaster gives you a clear view of what is coming in and when, so you are not guessing at month-end.

1️⃣ Shows you the gap between invoiced and collected, week by week.
2️⃣ Flags the months where cash gets tight before they arrive.
3️⃣ Takes one session to set up and saves a lot of late-night stress.

Here is the link: [link]

If the numbers look uncomfortable when you run it, that is useful information. Happy to go through it with you.

Pam
```

_Send as the second VIP nurture message, roughly two weeks after the Profit Maximiser. Follow up a few days later with the Cashflow Maximiser: five methods and more than 25 tactics for improving cash flow in a trade business._

