### Nurture rhythm

- **Week 2:** Send the Profit Maximiser. First VIP nurture message. Give away the tool. No pitch. Follow up three days later with the five-step expense review question: does this cost help me get or keep customers?
- **Week 5:** Send the Cashflow Forecaster. Second VIP nurture message. Keep the personalisation specific to something current, a wet quarter, a sector squeeze, late payment news in the trade. Follow up a few days later with the Cashflow Maximiser.
- **Week 8:** Send the Time Value Tracker. Third VIP nurture message. This one tends to land when Steve is visibly busy. If he has posted on LinkedIn about a difficult week or a big scheme, reference it in the personalisation line.
- **Day 90:** Check-in call or message. One short message. Reference something specific: a post he made, a project you noticed, or the tool you sent. Ask one question. Is now a better time for a quick call, or is the timing still off? If still no, move to a monthly post share and revisit at six months.

===== sales:value (78s, 11659 chars)
The Quick Wins Profit Maximiser is Pam's free 30-minute session for MDs of groundworks and allied trades businesses. Steve walks away knowing exactly where the margin is leaking, which lever to pull first, and what fixing it is worth.

- **Name:** The Quick Wins Profit Maximiser
- **Length:** 30 minutes
- **Who it is for:** MDs of groundworks, civils, drainage and allied trades businesses in Yorkshire, turning over £1.5M to £8M, winning decent work but not keeping enough of it
- **What they get:** A plain look at where the margin is going, which of the five profit levers is capping the business right now, and two or three things they can act on straight away
- **The promise:** No fluff. No pitch. No folder full of worksheets. Just a straight conversation about your numbers and where to start.

### The questions

| Area | Question | Red looks like | Green looks like |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Margin visibility | When you finish a job, can you tell me within a week what margin you actually made against what you priced? | He finds out at year-end, or not at all. Month-end numbers come from the accountant weeks later. | He reviews job margin within a week of completion and can spot the variance before the next job starts. |
| Pricing and pipeline | How far ahead is your tender pipeline right now, and do you have protected time each week to price work properly? | Pricing happens between site visits, in the van, in his head. There is no pipeline visible beyond the next two or three jobs. | He has a rolling six-month pipeline and a fixed half-day each week blocked out for estimating and business development. |
| Cash flow | At the end of a busy month, does the bank account reflect the turnover you are putting through? | Turnover looks fine but cash is tight. He is waiting on retentions, chasing main contractors past 60 days, and worrying about making payroll. | Cash flow is predictable. He knows what is coming in and when. Retentions are tracked and chased systematically. |
| Variation control | When variations come up on a job, are they written down and agreed before the work is done? | Variations are agreed verbally on site and half of them never get invoiced. The margin was there at pricing. It disappeared somewhere in the middle. | Every variation is recorded, agreed in writing with the main contractor and invoiced as a matter of course. |
| Team structure | When something goes wrong on site, who is the first person that gets the call? | His phone. Always. Even when his contracts manager is on site. He ends up sorting it himself because it is quicker. | His contracts manager or foreman handles it and brings him a solution, not a problem. He finds out after it is sorted. |
| Delegation | Is there anything running in the business right now that could only stop if you personally sorted it? | Yes, and he can name three or four things without pausing. The business is held together by him being in it all day every day. | He can take a week away and trust the business to keep moving. His team knows what to do and who decides what. |
| Profit awareness | Do you know which jobs or which clients are making you the most money, and which are costing you? | He has a gut feeling but no numbers to back it up. He takes whatever work he can win without knowing which is worth winning. | He can name his best and worst margin jobs by type and client. He uses that to price and choose work more deliberately. |
| Owner's return | If you worked out what the business actually pays you per hour for the risk and graft you carry, what would that number be? | He has never done the maths. Or he has, and the number was not what it should be for 15 years of graft and the personal exposure he carries. | He knows the number, it reflects the risk, and he is comfortable with it. The business pays him back properly for what he puts in. |
| Business dependency | If you stepped away from the business for a month, what would break? | Most things. Pricing, client relationships, cash decisions, site problems. The business cannot run without him in the middle of it. | The day-to-day keeps moving. Pricing and client relationships need him, but the rest has people around it who know what they are doing. |

### Your opening questions

1. Right now, what is the biggest thing stopping the business from being where it should be? Whether that's margin, the team, cash, or just the fact that everything still lands on you - what's the one that keeps you up at three in the morning?
2. If we sat down together this time next year and you said, 'Pam, we've cracked it' - what would need to have changed? More margin in the bank? The lads sorting their own problems? Proper weekends? Tell me what 'cracked it' actually looks like for you.
3. Beyond the numbers, why does sorting this matter to you right now? What does it actually cost you when it stays as it is - at home, not just in the business?
4. What have you already tried? Whether that's bringing in a contracts manager, going to a seminar, or putting your prices up - what's happened so far, and what got in the way of it sticking?

### The magic question

**The magic question**

```text
Based on what you've told me, I can see exactly where the margin is going and I've got a couple of ideas that could make a real difference to you quickly. Would you like me to share them?
```

_Wait for the yes. Do not move on until you have it. Then go into the bridge below._

**Bridge into the offer**

```text
Right. Based on what you've told me - that the margin looks fine when you price the job but there's next to nowt left by the time you finish it, that your contracts manager is a good lad but you're still doing half his job, and that you're missing the things at home that matter - here is how I work with MDs in exactly your situation.

First, we find out where the margin is actually going. Not a guess, not a feeling. We look at your numbers, your jobs, your pricing, and we find the leak. Most owners find something fixable in the first two or three weeks.

Second, we sort the team structure so the decisions that are landing on your phone stop landing there. That is not about hiring more people. It is about being clear on who owns what and holding them to it. Your contracts manager can take the pressure off - but only if the structure around him is right.

Third, we free up your time for the work that actually moves the business forward - pricing the next six months, building the right relationships with main contractors, choosing the jobs worth winning. Right now that time is being spent sorting plant breakdowns. That needs to change.

I've spent 43 years in business and the last 8 working specifically with MDs of groundworks and allied trades businesses in Yorkshire. I'm not coming in with theory. I know what a wrongly priced job looks like on the numbers, I know what retentions and 90-day payment terms do to cash flow, and I know what it costs to carry a business like this on your own shoulders for 15 years.

Does that sound like the kind of support that would make a real difference for you?
```

_Slow down through the three steps. Pause after each one and check it landed. The personal driver (home, family, the lad's football) should be reflected back in your own words, not quoted back word for word. Add John Davy's proof point here once a specific number is available - see the note in the proof summary._

